use std
into portrait.amni

Imports: os, math, gfx, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.shapes, gfx.scene3d, gfx.hair (the library hair module, used as `hair::`) and look (the local module look.amni, whose `look_setup(asset)` is used unqualified). This program renders a studio head-and-shoulders portrait of a companion character (Holo or Dex) with the Helium renderer, framed like the OpenMOSS face picture `art/holo_face.png`, so the two can be compared pixel for pixel.

`fn num(s const byte[:]) real` returns the value of `parse_flt(s)` (ignore its ok flag).

`fn tunes(s const byte[:], out f32[>..]&)` splits s on spaces and pushes `num(word) as f32` for every non-empty word. `fn tv(t const f32[:], i int, d f32) f32` returns t[i] when i < t.len, else d.

`fn mesh_node(asset int, name const byte[:]) int` returns the global index of the first node n of the asset (from `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first` for `.count` nodes) whose `gltf::nodes[n].mesh >= 0` and whose mesh name `gltf::text_of(gltf::meshes[gltf::nodes[n].mesh].name)` equals name, else -1.

`fn mat_color(asset int, name const byte[:]) float3` returns the xyz of `gltf::materials[m].base` for the first material m of the asset (`gltf::assets[asset].materials.first` for `.count`) whose `gltf::text_of(gltf::materials[m].name)` equals name, else `float3 { 0.6, 0.45, 0.25 }`.

`fn eye_pair(asset int) float3, float3` finds the node of the mesh named "eyes" with mesh_node and averages the positions of the vertices of every primitive of that mesh (`gltf::meshes[m].prims.first` for `.count`; each `gltf::prims[p]` has `vfirst` and `vcount`; position x, y, z of vertex v are `gltf::verts[v * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS + gltf::AT_POS + 0, 1, 2]`), separately for vertices with x > 0 and with x <= 0, and returns the two means (x > 0 first). When the node is missing it returns `float3 { 0.031, 1.51, 0.11 }` and `float3 { -0.031, 1.51, 0.11 }`.

`fn rect(pos float3, at float3, axis float3, w f32, h f32, rgb float3, sh bool)` calls `s3d::add_rect(pos, at, axis, w, h, rgb, 7.0, sh)`.

`main()`: `a = args()`; a[1] the .glb path, a[2] the output .png path, a[3] and a[4] width and height (`num(..) as int`), a[5] frames (`num(..) as int`), and when `a.len > 6` the tuning list `tunes(a[6], t)` into `var t f32[>..]`. Tuning indices and defaults: 0 key 1.0, 1 fill 0.45, 2 sky 0.8, 3 exposure 1.45, 4 fov 0.3, 5 eye spacing as a share of the width 0.241, 6 eye row from the top as a share of the height 0.408, 7 lens aperture 0.0003, 8 strands per guide 24, 9 frizz 0.7, 10 backdrop gray 0.55, 11 hair gain 1.7, 12 rim 2.0, 13 face roughness (0 keeps it), 14 hair color multiplier 1.0, 15 pore relief (0 keeps it), 16 skin rim (below 0 keeps it, default -1), 17 skin color desaturation 0, 18 skin scatter width scale 1.0, 19 body skin roughness (0 keeps it), 20 eye roughness (0 keeps it), 21 hide the eye occlusion shell when above 0, 22 hair style 1.0 (above 0 grows an updo on the scalp, 0 uses the model's hair cards), 23 updo guide count 2500.
1. `gfx::open_headless(w, h)`; on failure print "gpu: skipped (no device: " plus `gfx::error()` plus ")" and return. `asset = gltf::load(a[1])`; when negative print "load failed: " plus `gltf::error()` and return. Call `look_setup(asset)`. When tuning 13 is above 0, set `.roughness` to it on every material of the asset named "face", and when tuning 19 is above 0 set `.roughness` to it on every material named "skin". Then for every material x of the asset named "skin" or "face": when tuning 15 is above 0 set `x.shade_c.y` to it; when tuning 16 is at least 0 set `x.shade_c.z` to it; multiply `x.shade_a.x`, `.y` and `.z` by tuning 18; and with k tuning 17 and mean m of the rgb of `x.base`, set each of `x.base.x`, `.y`, `.z` to `c + (m - c) * k` for its own value c. When tuning 20 is above 0 set `.roughness` to it on every material named "eyes". When tuning 21 is above 0 set `gltf::nodes[n].mesh = -1` for every node of the asset whose mesh is named "eye_occ".
2. `let ep, en = eye_pair(asset)`; `mid = (ep + en) * 0.5`; `ipd = linalg::length3(ep - en)`; `fov = tv(t, 4, 0.3)`; `th = linalg::f(tan((fov * 0.5) as real))`; `d = ipd / (tv(t, 5, 0.241) * 2.0 * th * (w as f32) / (h as f32))`; `fh = 2.0 * d * th`; `at = mid - float3 { 0.0, (0.5 - tv(t, 6, 0.408)) * fh, 0.0 }`. Print "portrait: eyes " ep, en, " spacing ", ipd, " distance ", d.
3. Backdrop: `var bm = shapes::material(linalg::v3(g, g * 0.985, g * 1.03), 0.0, 0.95)` with g tuning 10, `bm.double_sided = true`; `let bs = shapes::sheet(7, mid + float3 { -2.5, -2.5, -1.4 }, float3 { 5.0, 0.0, 0.0 }, float3 { 0.0, 5.0, 0.0 }, bm)`; `s3d::place(s3d::add(bs), gfx::mat4_identity)`.
4. `s3d::alpha_to_coverage = true`; `s3d::init(w, h)`. Hair: `var gs int[>..]`; `let sn = mesh_node(asset, "scalp")`. When tuning 22 (default 1.0) is above 0 and sn >= 0, grow an updo: `var pos f32[>..] = []`, `var nrm f32[>..] = []`, `var idx int[>..] = []`; `hair::mesh_soup(sn, pos, nrm, idx)`; `top` is the largest y over the vertices (y of vertex i is `pos[i * 3 + 1]`); over the vertices with y above `top - 0.14` take the smallest and largest x (`x0`, `x1`) and z (`z0`, `z1`); `rx = (x1 - x0) * 0.5`, `rz = (z1 - z0) * 0.5`, `ry = (rx + rz) * 0.5`, `r = max(rx, max(ry, rz))`, `c = float3 { (x0 + x1) * 0.5, top - ry, (z0 + z1) * 0.5 }`, `sc = float3 { rx / r, ry / r, rz / r }`. Main groom: `var rp float3[>..] = []`, `var rn float3[>..] = []`; `let _ = hair::mesh_roots(sn, float3 { -1.0, top - 0.19, -1.0 }, float3 { 1.0, top + 1.0, 1.0 }, tv(t, 23, 2500.0) as int, 7, rp, rn)`; `let dir = linalg::unit3(float3 { 0.0, 0.6, -0.8 })`; `let st = hair::Style { steps: 24, length: 0.3, width: 0.004, clumps: 300, head: float4 { c.x, c.y, c.z, r }, head_scale: sc, gather: c + float3 { dir.x * sc.x, dir.y * sc.y, dir.z * sc.z } * (r * 1.08), bun: 0.032, .. }`; `var data byte[>..] = []`; `hair::groom(rp[..], rn[..], st, data)`; push `hair::load(data[..], tv(t, 8, 24.0) as int)` when it is at least 0. Temple wisps: `var wp float3[>..] = []`, `var wn float3[>..] = []`; `let _ = hair::mesh_roots(sn, float3 { -1.0, c.y - 0.01, c.z + rz * 0.35 }, float3 { 1.0, c.y + ry * 0.55, 1.0 }, 160, 11, wp, wn)`; copy into `var qp float3[>..] = []` and `var qn float3[>..] = []` only the roots whose `abs(p.x - c.x) > rx * 0.6`; when qp.len > 0 grow them with `hair::groom(qp[..], qn[..], hair::Style { steps: 16, length: 0.17, vary: 0.35, width: 0.003, droop: 0.95, curl: 0.25, turns: 1.5, seed: 11, .. }, wdata)` into `var wdata byte[>..] = []` and push `hair::load(wdata[..], 7)` when it is at least 0. For every pushed groom g set `hair::grooms[g].look.color = float3 { 0.42, 0.32, 0.22 } * tv(t, 14, 1.0)`, `.look.gain = tv(t, 11, 1.7)`, `.look.frizz = tv(t, 9, 0.7)`, `.look.ambient = float3 { 1.2, 1.2, 1.2 }`. Then set `gltf::nodes[n].mesh = -1` for every node n of the asset whose mesh is named "hair", "braid_hair" or "tie" (loop over the asset's nodes, checking `mesh >= 0` before reading the name). Otherwise (tuning 22 at 0 or no scalp) use the model's hair cards: for each name in "hair" and "braid_hair": `let g = hair::cards(asset, name, "head", tv(t, 8, 24.0) as int)`; when g >= 0 push it and set `hair::grooms[g].look.color = mat_color(asset, name) * tv(t, 14, 1.0)`, `.look.gain = tv(t, 11, 1.7)`, `.look.frizz = tv(t, 9, 0.7)`, `.look.ambient = float3 { 1.2, 1.2, 1.2 }`, then set `gltf::nodes[n].mesh = -1` for every node n of the asset whose mesh is named name. Print "portrait: grooms " gs.len.
5. `let item = s3d::add(asset)`; `s3d::place(item, gfx::mat4_identity)`; for each g in gs `hair::follow(g, item, -1)`.
6. Lights: `s3d::sun = s3d::Sun { to_sun: linalg::unit3(float3 { 0.5, 0.6, 0.6 }), rgb: float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }, shadows: false, shadow_far: 10.0, map_size: 2048, .. }`. `rect(mid + float3 { 0.55, 0.45, 0.9 }, mid, float3 { 0.85, 0.0, -0.52 }, 0.9, 0.9, float3 { 10.0, 9.6, 9.0 } * tv(t, 0, 1.0), true)`; `rect(mid + float3 { -0.8, 0.1, 0.8 }, mid, float3 { 0.7, 0.0, 0.7 }, 1.0, 1.0, float3 { 0.9, 1.0, 1.1 } * tv(t, 1, 0.45), false)`; `rect(mid + float3 { -0.5, 0.35, -0.8 }, mid, float3 { 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 }, 0.3, 0.7, float3 { 4.0, 3.5, 2.9 } * tv(t, 12, 2.0), true)`. `s3d::sky = s3d::Sky { zenith: float3 { 0.5, 0.5, 0.52 }, horizon: float3 { 0.55, 0.55, 0.57 }, ground: float3 { 0.2, 0.19, 0.18 }, ambient: tv(t, 2, 0.8), draw: false }`.
7. Look: `s3d::look.curve = 2` (AgX keeps skin from going orange), `.bloom = 0.03`, `.ssao = true`, `s3d::ao_mode = 1`, `.ao_radius = 0.04`, `.ao_power = 1.2`, `.saturation = 1.1`, `.contrast = 1.15`, `.sharpen = 0.6`, `.exposure = tv(t, 3, 1.45)`; `s3d::sss_on = true`; `s3d::temporal = 1`.
8. Camera: `s3d::view = s3d::Camera { eye: at + float3 { 0.0, 0.0, d }, target: at, up: float3 { 0.0, 1.0, 0.0 }, fov_y: fov, near: 0.05, far: 30.0 }`; `s3d::dof_aperture = tv(t, 7, 0.0003)`; `s3d::dof_focus = d`.
9. `let target = gfx::render_target(w, h, gfx::RGBA8)`. For k below frames: when gs.len > 0, if `s3d::render_begin()` then `hair::draw(0.0)` and `s3d::render_end(target)`; else `s3d::render(target)`; then `gfx::flush()`. Save with `gfx::save_png(target, a[2])`, print "portrait: saved " and that result, then `gfx::close()`.
